@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Agent version pin — the image carries the release imagebuild resolved, and says so

' purpose: why `@latest` in a Dockerfile is not a pin, and the one shape every harness uses instead
' covers: internal/imagebuild/imagebuild.go (AgentVersion)
' covers: internal/imagebuild/targets.go (Spec.Pins)
' covers: internal/imagebuild/imagebuild_test.go
' covers: defs/*/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/claudecode/mcp/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/claudecode/solidity/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/*/test.sh
' covers: defs/*/tests/test_build.sh
' covers: internal/contract/agent_pin_test.go
' covers: internal/posture/posture.go (AgentVersion)
' covers: internal/run/run.go
'
' ── THE PIN NEEDS A GAUGE, BECAUSE THE UPDATER USED TO BE ONE ──────────────
' Every harness now switches its CLI's own auto-updater OFF — claudecode by
' DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER, opencode by OPENCODE_AUTO_UPDATE, cecli by
' `check-update: false`, cursor by the same class. That is correct: a
' SUCCESSFUL update replaces the release imagebuild resolved and unpins the image.
'
' But it moved the update layer UP, and the rung it moved to has no motor and
' had no dial. Measured rather than assumed:
'
'   upstream → image    `Builder.AgentVersion` resolves the newest release AT
'                       BUILD TIME and passes it as a build-arg. A real update
'                       mechanism — but it turns only when a human runs
'                       `proveo build <target>`. There is no CI in this repo and
'                       no scheduled rebuild.
'   image → sbx store   SELF-INVALIDATING, and this was the surprise: sbx.
'                       HasTemplate compares the local docker image ID against a
'                       receipt and reloads on mismatch, so a rebuild does reach
'                       the sandbox without PROVEO_SBX_RELOAD.
'   store → container   a fresh sandbox per run. Nothing stale can survive here.
'
' SO THE ONLY SLACK IS THE TOP RUNG, and before this line existed it was silent:
' the failing updater was never updating anything, but it did announce once per
' run that a version was fixed. Removing it left a months-old image
' indistinguishable from a fresh one. `proveo.agent.version` was already LABELed
' on the image and asserted by the def tests — the fact was carried and never
' delivered.
'
' THE RUN NOW DELIVERS IT. posture.AgentVersion reads the label and run.go
' prints one line under the `run` section. Best-effort by design: an sbx-only
' host has no docker to inspect with, docker prints `<no value>` for a missing
' label, and in every such case the line is OMITTED rather than guessed. A gauge
' that lies is worse than one that is absent, and neither may fail a run.
'
' STILL OPEN: nothing compares the pinned version against what upstream now
' publishes, so the line says WHICH version, never whether it is old. That check
' needs a network call proveo does not otherwise make at run time.
'
' ── TWO PINNING SHAPES, AND WHEN EACH IS USED ──────────────────────────────
' A. RESOLVED AT BUILD TIME, bare ARG, no default — the AGENT itself, whose
'    release moves constantly and whose version the image LABELs:
'    CECLI_VERSION, SERENA_VERSION, OPENCODE_VERSION, CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION,
'    CURSOR_AGENT_VERSION. Each is a `Pin` on its target's Specs row
'    (internal/imagebuild/targets.go); BuildTarget calls Builder.AgentVersion
'    and passes the result in; the Dockerfile guards with `test -n` and
'    verifies with the tool's own --version.
'    (proveo_agent_version in defs/lib/docker-build.sh until 2026-09-22.)
'
'    FINDING: codex is NOT pinned. Its row carries CODEX_VERSION as an EnvArg
'    defaulting to `latest`, not a Pin — the shell build.sh did the same, and
'    the P2 port kept parity. agent_pin_test does not cover codex, so nothing
'    fails when the codex image bakes whatever is newest.
' B. PINNED IN THE DOCKERFILE as an ARG WITH a default — supporting toolchains
'    that move on their own cadence and are not the image's headline agent:
'      PLANTUML_VERSION=1.2026.6      NODE_MAJOR=22       pnpm@10.33.0
'      BUN_VERSION=1.4.0 (+ SHA256)   PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION=1.61.0
'      AGENT_BROWSER_VERSION=0.36.0 (+ SHA256)
'      SEMGREP_VERSION=1.176.1        SOLC_SELECT_VERSION=1.2.0
'      SOLHINT_VERSION=6.2.4          FOUNDRY_VERSION=v1.8.1
'      MITMPROXY_VERSION=11.1.3
'    The CACHE argument is identical in both shapes and is the whole reason for
'    either: BuildKit caches a RUN by its text, so an unpinned line re-runs only
'    when something ABOVE it changes, and a warm cache keeps last year's linter
'    with nothing in the build saying so.
'
' Still unpinned, and known to be (see _spec/_plans/image-size-reduction.puml
' item (c) for the full list): the six LSP servers in base-node-lsp, and the
' codex agent (CODEX_VERSION=latest, above).
' The lsp-builder go installs and plantuml-lsp are pinned
' (_spec/internal/maintain/build-schedule.puml).
'
' ── TWO PINS IN ONE IMAGE: cecli ───────────────────────────────────────────
' cecli resolves BOTH cecli-dev and serena-agent through the same resolver, into
' separate venvs. Serena is pinned for the reason the layer was deleted the FIRST
' time: an unpinned `pip install serena-agent` shipped a version skew that left
' the MCP server dead on every boot for weeks.
'
' ── FOUNDRY: WHY A DEFAULTED ARG WAS STILL AN UPGRADE ──────────────────────
' `curl -L https://foundry.paradigm.xyz | bash && foundryup` installs the
' NIGHTLY — foundryup with no argument takes latest, and foundry's release feed is
' nightlies with a stable tag every few weeks. So the image's toolchain changed
' under it on EVERY rebuild, which is precisely what this diagram exists to
' prevent. `foundryup --install <tag>` pins it; `forge --version` and
' `cast --version` make the pin a fact about the image.
'
' ── WHY A PIN AT ALL: BuildKit's CACHE KEY ─────────────────────────────────
' The `pin:` note states the version AND how it was chosen. The first version of
' it said "from CECLI_VERSION" on every build, because it was printed by the
' caller — which assigns the result to a variable named like the override, after
' which "override or resolved" is unknowable from outside.
'
' BuildKit caches a RUN layer by its text and its parents. An
' `npm install -g x@latest` line NEVER changes when upstream publishes, so a
' warm cache keeps last month's agent and nothing in the build says so.
' Resolving the release ahead of the build and passing it as a --build-arg the
' Dockerfile USES moves the cache key exactly when upstream moves, and lets the
' image LABEL what it carries.
'
' A default on the ARG would be the same hole one step removed — a bare
' `docker build` would bake whatever is newest — so the ARG is declared bare
' and guarded by a version check inside the same RUN.
'
' One row per agent, uniform across ecosystems:
'   npm     the registry's dist-tag `latest` for the package
'   pypi    PyPI's current release
'   cursor  the build cursor.com/install unpacks, read out of the script
'           itself: the installer hardcodes its release and takes no version
'           argument, so that script is the only place the version is stated.
'           An image without the label predates the pin — rebuild rather than
'           trust it.
'
' An exported override wins outright (OPENCODE_VERSION=1.18.20 proveo build
' opencode): that is how a maintainer rebuilds an older agent, or builds
' offline. AgentVersion returns the bare version; the `pin:` note (ui.Notef)
' saying which release is about to be baked, and how it was chosen, prints
' beside the buildx line — inside the resolver rather than by the caller,
' because only the resolver knows whether the value came from the override or
' from upstream. The shell learned that the hard way: its caller assigned the
' result to a variable named like the override, after which nothing outside
' could tell an override from a resolution.

actor "Maintainer" as MT
participant "cmd/proveo build\n(internal/maintain BuildPlan)" as CMD <<app>>
participant "imagebuild.BuildTarget\ntargets.go Specs[<target>].Pins" as BUILD <<app>>
participant "imagebuild.go\nBuilder.AgentVersion" as LIB <<app>>
participant "upstream release source\nnpm dist-tag · PyPI · cursor.com/install" as UP <<cloud>>
participant "BuildKit\ndefs/<harness>/Dockerfile" as BK <<host>>
database "image labels\nproveo.agent · proveo.agent.version" as LBL <<db>>
participant "internal/contract\nagent_pin_test.go" as CT <<app>>

MT ARROW_MAIN CMD : " ""proveo build <target>"" "
CMD ARROW_MAIN BUILD : " ""imagebuild <target> --tag local"" (in-process) "
BUILD ARROW_MAIN LIB : " ""AgentVersion(<ARG>, <eco>, <pkg>)"" "
alt <ARG> exported by the maintainer
  LIB ARROW_OPTIONAL BUILD : that version, upstream not asked
else resolve
  LIB ARROW_CLOUD UP : npm registry JSON · PyPI JSON · read the installer
  UP ARROW_CLOUD LIB : current release
  LIB ARROW_MAIN BUILD : version
else unreachable
  LIB ARROW_ERROR BUILD : errorLabel(refuse — names ""<ARG>=<x.y.z>"" as the way out)
end
BUILD ARROW_MAIN MT : " ""pin: <pkg>@<version> (resolved upstream; override with <ARG>=…)"" "
BUILD ARROW_MAIN BK : " ""--build-arg <ARG>=<version>"" "
BK ARROW_MAIN BK : " ""ARG <ARG>"" (bare) → ""test -n"" guard\n→ install ""<pkg>@${<ARG>}"" → ""<agent> --version"" = ""${<ARG>}"" "
BK ARROW_MAIN LBL : " ""LABEL proveo.agent=<pkg> proveo.agent.version=${<ARG>}"" "
CT ARROW_OPTIONAL BK : every harness row: bare ARG · guard · pinned install\n· label · no ""@latest"" spelling
CT ARROW_OPTIONAL LIB : imagebuild_test.go, fake Fetch: npm · PyPI\n· installer scrape · override · refusal

note over BK
  **""@latest"" is a request to the registry, not a property of the image.**
  BuildKit caches a ""RUN"" by its text and its parent chain. An
  ""npm install -g x@latest"" line does not change when upstream publishes,
  so with a warm cache the layer is reused and the image keeps the agent
  from whenever that layer last ran — with nothing in the build saying so.
  Only ""--no-cache"", or an edit ABOVE the line, ever refreshed it. The
  fleet was current on 2026-09-01 by coincidence: unrelated Dockerfile edits
  had busted the cache that morning, while the published
  ""opencode-browser:latest"" from a week earlier still carried 1.18.23.

  **A used build-arg is what moves the cache key.** BuildKit misses the cache
  on an ARG's first //use// when its value differs from the previous build,
  never on its declaration. So the version must appear in the install
  line itself; a label alone would record a version the layer did not
  necessarily install.

  **Bare ""ARG"", by design.** A default would be ""@latest"" one step removed:
  a bare ""docker build"" would bake whatever is newest and label it
  truthfully, so the guard fails that build instead and points at
  ""proveo build <target>"".

  **The version check makes the pin a fact about the image.**
  ""opencode --version"" must print the arg exactly;
  ""claude --version"" must contain it; ""pip show"" must report it;
  cursor's installer, which takes no version, must have unpacked the
  release ""AgentVersion"" read out of the script.
end note

note over LIB
  **One resolver, three ecosystems, one shape.** ""npm"" reads
  ""<registry>/<pkg>/latest"" from the registry JSON (""NPM_CONFIG_REGISTRY""
  honoured; the shell's ""npm view"" is gone, so the host needs no npm);
  ""pypi"" reads ""info.version"" over Go's HTTP client, so no curl or
  python3 on the host is involved; ""cursor"" scrapes the release directory out of
  ""cursor.com/install"", because that script hardcodes its build and takes no
  argument. The maintainer override is the same ""<ARG>"" the Dockerfile
  takes, so ""OPENCODE_VERSION=1.18.20 proveo build opencode"" pins an older
  release and doubles as the offline path. A failed fetch is still a
  refusal, never a guessed version; the ""Failf"" line names
  ""<ARG>=<x\.y\.z>"" and the last fetch error.

  **Unresolvable is a refusal, never a silent ""latest"".** Falling back to the
  dist-tag would reopen the hole in the one place — offline, behind a
  proxy — where nobody is watching the build log. It returns an error and
  no version when it fails, because a caller would bake whatever it got,
  and the ""Failf"" line names ""<ARG>=<x\.y\.z>"" as the way out. An ecosystem
  it does not know is refused the same way ("unknown ecosystem"), rather
  than being treated as one of the three it does.
end note

note over LBL
  **How to know what a harness carries:**
  ""docker image inspect -f '{{index .Config.Labels "proveo.agent.version"}}' proveo/opencode:local""
  and what upstream ships: ""npm view opencode-ai version"". The build log's
  ""pin:"" line states the same version at build time. Each def's runtime suite
  (""test_build.sh"") checks the label against the agent's own ""--version"".
end note

@enduml
